<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siemowit II of Masovia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1283–<a href="/years/1345">1345</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Siemowit II of Rawa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siemowit II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> was a Polish duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-of-masovia">Bolesław II of Masovia</a>. When his father died in 1313 the Masovian lands were divided among the brothers, and Siemowit received the southern part around Rawa, which he ruled until his death in 1345.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siemowit-ii-of-masovia.jpg" alt="Siemowit II of Masovia" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous plate · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ziemowit_II_Mazowiecki_seal.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Siemowit was the eldest son of Bolesław II of Płock and his first wife Gaudemantė, or Sophia, a daughter of Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traidenis">Traidenis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and was probably named after his paternal grandfather Siemowit I. In 1310, before his father's death, he received the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and Liw. In the division of 1313 he took Rawa Mazowiecka as his capital, with Sochaczew, Zakroczym, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gostynin">Gostynin</a>, Ciechanów and Wizna, while his younger brothers Trojden I and Wenceslaus received Czersk and Płock. The settlement satisfied none of them, and in 1316 the three fought a brief war whose details are unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Between his powerful neighbours, King Władysław I the Elbow-high of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, Siemowit often changed sides. With Władysław he helped place his nephew Bolesław Jerzy II on the throne of Halych in 1323, and in 1325 the Masovian dukes joined Władysław's coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. When Wenceslaus allied with the Teutonic Order and Władysław plundered Płock, Siemowit and Trojden made their own alliance with the Order at Brodnica on 2 January 1326, which guaranteed their independence; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> then invaded his lands.</p><p>In 1329 the brothers unexpectedly fought for Władysław against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> in Kujawy, and a year later Siemowit obtained neutrality. In 1333 he refused the Order's offer of Brześć Kujawski and took Władysław's side for good. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kalisz-1343">Treaty of Kalisz</a> of 8 July 1343, which made peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the Order, eased the position of his duchy, and as a possible successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> he waived his rights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> and Eastern Pomerania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Siemowit died in February 1345 and was buried either in Płock Cathedral, as Jan Długosz reports, or in the Dominican monastery of Warka. He never married and had no children, so his duchy was divided among his three surviving nephews, Bolesław III, Siemowit III and Casimir I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siemowit II of Masovia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Boleslaw II of Masovia died in 1313, his lands were divided among his sons, and <b>Siemowit II</b> received the southern part around Rawa.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-rawa">Duchy of Rawa</a></li></ul></section>
